n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"Fe2+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Iron(2+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Ferrous ion\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/Fe/q+2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"H+\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Hydrogen(1+)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Aqueous proton\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/p+1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic photoreduction supplying formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_001\",\n        \"mol_003\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_005\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Cu(I)-cyanide complexes: photoredox catalyst\",\n        \"Ultraviolet radiation: energy source\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)-cyanide complexes\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-irradiated, cyanide-bearing water with sulfide/HS-; proposed environmental window rather than an exact reproduction of published conditions. Copper availability, sulfide precipitation, and UV attenuation constrain operation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net two-electron reduction of HCN gives an imine-level intermediate whose hydrolysis supplies formaldehyde and ammonia. Sulfide supplies reducing equivalents. Elemental sulfur is an idealized oxidized-sulfur endpoint; the balanced reference equation is 8 HCN + 8 H2S + 8 H2O -> 8 CH2O + 8 NH3 + S8.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) supports cyanide/sulfide photoredox chemistry feeding carbonyl and aminonitrile synthesis. This node is a coarse-grained formaldehyde-supply reaction, not a claim of selective isolation of formaldehyde. Actual oxidized sulfur includes condition-dependent sulfur and polysulfide pools, so the S8 endpoint is bookkeeping rather than a measured universal product distribution. Rapid downstream trapping competes with further photochemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate anaerobic photocatalytic dehydrogenation of methanol\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_006\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_007\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"ZnS semiconductor surface: candidate photocatalyst\",\n        \"Ultraviolet radiation: energy source\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ZnS semiconductor surface, potentially sphalerite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed anoxic, methanol-bearing mineral-water interface irradiated above the semiconductor band-gap energy; hydrogen escape and rapid formaldehyde capture are favorable. Natural-mineral activity must be established.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photogenerated holes oxidize adsorbed methanol toward formaldehyde while conduction-band electrons support proton reduction to H2. The ideal net transformation is CH3OH -> CH2O + H2; molecular oxygen is unnecessary.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a prebiotic network edge: none of the supplied papers demonstrates this methanol/ZnS route under the stated environmental conditions. Semiconductor-assisted alcohol dehydrogenation supplies a chemical precedent, but activity and selectivity of natural ZnS cannot be assumed from engineered photocatalysts. This branch tests a chemically different C1 feedstock; photocorrosion, further oxidation, and insufficient methanol concentration are major limitations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Imine-entry Strecker formation of aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated, ammonia-bearing aqueous microenvironment, approximately pH 7-9; HCN retention and carbonyl capture are required. Avoid prolonged unprotected UV exposure after aminonitrile formation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia reversibly form an imine/iminium pool, followed by cyanide addition to produce aminoacetonitrile. The node represents the net condensation and C-C bond-forming addition.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) supports Strecker-type aminonitrile chemistry in a cyanosulfidic context. This is a direct, chemically economical route to the glycine carbon skeleton. Formaldehyde hydration, cyanohydrin formation, HCN volatilization, and competing oligomerization influence the yield; no mineral catalyst is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde trapping as glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_001\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous solution, approximately pH 7-9, with sufficient dissolved HCN/CN-; reversible trapping, not irreversible quantitative sequestration.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to the formaldehyde carbonyl, followed by proton transfer, forming the cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) supports cyanohydrin intermediates in cyanide-based carbonyl chemistry. Glycolonitrile is included because it is a chemically meaningful formaldehyde reservoir and competing entry state for aminonitrile production, not merely to create an additional connection. Cyanide is a reactant rather than an added mineral catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Net amination of the glycolonitrile reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_009\",\n        \"mol_004\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Ammonia-rich aqueous phase, approximately pH 8-10; cyanohydrin/carbonyl exchange and HCN retention must remain possible.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net replacement of the cyanohydrin oxygen functionality by an amino group yields aminoacetonitrile. This can proceed through reversible cyanohydrin dissociation and imine chemistry; an elementary, uncatalyzed displacement of hydroxide is not asserted.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) provides the relevant cyanohydrin/aminonitrile chemistry context. This is a reservoir-entry alternative to rxn_003, not an established independent elementary mechanism. Its kinetic separation from