1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Nominal pH 1-3 with potentially prolonged residence. Ammonia is predominantly ammonium under these conditions; neutral formulas express the reference net reaction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the activated amide carbonyl. Collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases the amide nitrogen as ammonia/ammonium and forms glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes the aminonitrile-to-amino-acid transformation discussed in Patel et al. (2015). The two-node representation exposes the amide-hydrolysis bottleneck rather than assuming instantaneous conversion. The supplied material does not establish rates or quantitative yields for this proposed surface-pool window; persistence and accumulation require kinetic testing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type carbonate capture and hydantoin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Concentrated ammoniacal carbonate/bicarbonate solution, nominal pH 8-10. CO2 denotes the supplied inorganic-carbon pool; carbonate speciation follows pH.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminonitrile carbamylation and cyclization through imino heterocyclic intermediates give hydantoin. The balanced net transformation is aminoacetonitrile + CO2 -> hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a prebiotic network edge: standard Bucherer-Bergs chemistry provides the chemical precedent, but none of the supplied studies demonstrates this carbonate-trapping branch in a realistic early-Earth setting. It is included as a genuine alternative chemical fate of the aminonitrile, potentially storing it in a cyclic ureide. Required ammonia/carbonate concentrations and competition with direct hydrolysis remain unestablished.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hot hydantoin hydrolysis and decarbamylation to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_013\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_021\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_012\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Aqueous proton catalysis\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423-453 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"25-100 bar, maintaining liquid water\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aqueous acid\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed acidic hydrothermal fluid, nominal room-temperature pH 1-3. Requires transport from the carbonate-rich formation environment, sufficient residence for hydrolysis, and subsequent cooling to limit glycine destruction.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential ring opening through N-carbamoylglycine and hydrolytic decarbamylation release glycine, ammonia, and CO2. This net reaction node combines those stages: hydantoin + 2 H2O -> glycine + NH3 + CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative as a geochemical pathway under the stated conditions: hydantoin hydrolysis is established synthetic chemistry, but the supplied papers do not validate this hydantoin-to-glycine environmental sequence. The demanding hydrolysis conditions are an explicit bottleneck. Qian et al. (1993) provides relevant high-temperature amino-acid stability context, not evidence for this particular synthesis. Glycine formation must outcompete its thermal decomposition; no thermodynamic-sink assumption is made.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Coarse-grained CO2-sulfite photoredox supply of glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_012\",\n        \"mol_014\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_015\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Ultraviolet radiation: energy source\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, CO2-bearing sulfite solution under UV irradiation; pH and sulfite/bisulfite speciation require control. Sulfite is a consumed electron donor, not a catalyst. Radical quenching by dissolved metals and product photodestruction can limit yield.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfite photochemistry supplies reducing equivalents for CO2 reduction and carbon-carbon bond formation in a multistep network. The idealized balanced endpoint is 2 CO2 + 2 SO3^2- + H2O -> glyoxylic acid + 2 SO4^2-.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Liu et al. (2021) supports a CO2/sulfite photoredox network supplying organic intermediates. This glyoxylate endpoint is a conditional coarse-graining, not a demonstrated single elementary reaction or a claim of selective quantitative production. The exclusive sulfate endpoint is an electron-balance assumption; actual sulfur products and carbon-product distributions require verification against the primary experiments. The supplied review's glyoxylate-hydrate dehydration is not treated as independent carbon synthesis or as intrinsically requiring photoredox chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Iron-driven glyoxylate oxime formation and reduction\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_017\",\n        \"mol_018\",\n        \"mol_020\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_021\",\n        \"mol_019\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Native iron: consumed reductant and reactive interface\",\n        \"Dissolved Fe(II): coordination promoter\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe(II) coordination assistance; Fe(0) is consumed\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic warm pool or geothermal porewater with locally available native or meteoritic iron, supplied hydroxylamine, and a proton-buffering reservoir. Native iron is not assumed ubiquitous; sulfide/phosphate passivation and hydroxylamine loss require assessment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine condenses with glyoxylate to an oxime. Reduction of the C=N functionality and N-O bond gives glycine. The reference balance is glyoxylic acid + NH2OH + 2 Fe + 4 H+ -> glycine + 2 Fe2+ + 2 H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska, Varma, and Moran (2019) supports iron-promoted conversion of keto-acid precursors to amino acids using hydroxylamine. Explicit Fe(0) oxidation and proton consumption repair the electron and hydrogen imbalance in the review's shorthand equation; Fe(II) alone is not an unlimited reductant. The 343 K experimental temperature is compatible with a surface geothermal pool, so high pressure is not imposed unnecessarily. Hydroxylamine availability is unresolved, and actual iron consumption can exceed the ideal electron balance because of corrosion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate ammonia-based reductive amination of glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\n        \"mol_016\",\n        \"mol_004\",\n        \"mol_018\",\n        \"mol_020\"\n      ],\n      \"outputs\": [\n        \"mol_021\",\n        \"mol_019\",\n        \"mol_002\"\n      ],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Native iron: candidate consumed reductant and reactive interface\",\n        \"Dissolved Fe(II): possible coordination promoter\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"343-370 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Candidate iron-surface and Fe(II) coordination assistance\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Proposed ammonia-rich, anoxic warm porewater, approximately pH 6-8, with accessible Fe(0) and proton buffering. Conditions require experimental validation; carbonyl reduction and iron passivation compete.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia forms a reversible imine/iminium pool with glyoxylate. A two-electron reduction could yield glycine. The balanced reference equation is glyoxylic acid + NH3 + Fe + 2 H+ -> glycine + Fe2+ + H2O.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative under the stated prebiotic conditions. Muchowska et al. (2019), as summarized in the supplied research, supports the hydroxylamine/iron branch and must not be cited as proving an interchangeable ammonia route. This candidate avoids the hydroxylamine feedstock bottleneck but introduces potentially unfavorable imine formation and reduction kinetics. Direct glyoxylate reduction, ammonia speciation, and surface passivation may dominate; glycine production and selectivity require measurement.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic carbon supply, imine-entry Strecker, amide hydrolysis\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN-derived formaldehyde enters Strecker chemistry directly, followed by sequential nitrile and amide hydrolysis. This is the principal literature-grounded route, subject to hydrolysis-rate and concentration constraints.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\n        \"rxn_001\",\n        \"rxn_003\",\n        \"rxn_006\",\n        \"rxn_007\"\n      ],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\n        \"mol_008\",\n        \"mol_010\",\n        \"mol_011\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic carbon supply through the cyanohydrin reservoir\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN-derived formaldehyde is first tr